From 11dcd2b4c9c285e5802ea5779440764a8041ea31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 15:40:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 128/206] fix(shorebird): three bugs the base-snapshot path had
 been hiding

Registering the base snapshots was enough to segfault at the first await.
Nothing on the host ever registered them, so everything gated on
BaseSnapshotsAreSet() had zero coverage until an iPhone ran it.

Resume stub jumped to address 0. Its have_base_range arm ends in
Jump(resume_on_{cpu,simulator}_entry_point), and the setters for both Thread
fields have no callers anywhere in the tree -- they are initialised to 0 and
never written. Control only ever reached the other arm, where
base_instructions_entry_point_ == 0 and the parked pc is jumped to directly.
The comment three lines up says the base_instructions pair "were never written
by anything; this is the writer"; the resume pair was missed. Write them in the
same place, from StubCode::ResumeOnCPU()/ResumeOnSimulator().

This also narrows the earlier "gen_kernel async crash is not a regression"
verdict: that investigation exercised the == 0 arm and was right about it. The
bug is in the arm it could not reach.

LinkOracle::ReadHeaderSize did not page-align. The value it returns is handed
to the loader as the ELF's file offset, and Dart_LoadELF rejects an offset that
is not page-aligned. tools/linker.py rounds up to 16384; the reader did not, so
for the b5 app's 8686 entries it answered 69492 while the ELF sat at 81920 --
pointing the loader at the zero pad. The header comment directly above the
function already documented the pad. Documentation right, code wrong, tests
following the code.

entry_count() derived the count from header_size_, which only worked while
header_size_ was exactly 4 + 8n. With the pad it answers 2047 for every table
that fits in a page. Read the stored count instead. This one is only reachable
once the alignment is fixed.

Two tests asserted the unpadded size, one of them in its own name
(ReadHeaderSizeIsCountTimesEight), and stayed green on the wrong
implementation because the fixture was wrong the same way: MakeHeader emitted
4 + 8n bytes with no pad and no ELF, so a correct reader and one that stops at
the end of the table were indistinguishable on it. Fixture now builds a real
vmcode prefix; add ReadHeaderSizeSpansSeveralPages for the multi-page case that
actually broke. The truncated-input case had to be re-cut too -- with a padded
fixture, dropping trailing bytes leaves the declared table inside the buffer
and proves nothing.

Add an FHP_SETBASE gate to dartaotruntime so the base-snapshot path has a host
repro at all. Same binary, same snapshot, one env var apart: unset exits 0,
set crashed with pc=0 before this change and exits 0 after.

LinkOracle|Shorebird: 34 passed, 0 failed. Note that run_vm_tests takes one
test name and silently ignores the rest, so a single invocation with 34 names
runs one of them and still exits 0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

